  /*!
    \page pyponca %Unsing Ponca in python

    Since 2.0, Ponca has an official python binding that supports most fonctionnalities the library has to offer. 


    \section pyponca Building the python extension

    For now, there is no official pypi repository. Ponca extension must be built manually. It is recommended to create
    a dedicated environement (see [venv](https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html) or [conda](https://anaconda.org/anaconda/conda)). 
    In order to build the extension, go to the PyPonca directory, and run the command `pip install .`. The building is quite long, have 
    some patience.

    \section pyponca_usage PyPonca usage

    \subsection pyponca_basics Basic usage

    % TODO : How do we list the methods ? 
    % Documentations ? 

    \subsection pyponca_arrays Array protocol

    PyPonca uses the [dlpack](https://dmlc.github.io/dlpack/latest/) array protocol for exchanging array data such as points and arrays. 
    This protocol is supported by most numeric libraries such as numpy, jax, pytorch, tensorflow and much more. Any function requiring data 
    can therefore work with arrays of the beformentionned libraries. 
    
    However, returned array are raw dlpack object that might need to be converted by the desired API. For instance, to obtain a numpy array 
    returned by `pyponca`, the returned object should be wrapped by `np.from_dlpack` call. In most cases this does not perform copy (unless 
    explicitely specified) and allow to maintain copyless compatibility with almost all libraries. 

    \section pyponca_cpp What differs from the C++ library

    Ponca heavily rely on template, which is incompatible with interpreted languages by essence. In order to ease the use of the library, 
    we provide some dispatch types that are under common names. The required information are infered through arguments provided and only
    when needed is the final type selected. 

    In the C++ version, the heavy computation happens when calling the `compute` method. In the python version computation happens when the 
    results are queried (for instance when calling the `potential` method). This is a consequence of the poor performance of iterating in 
    the python side and the dispatching described above. For this reason, the base point cloud for computation is provided through the 
    `attach` method, which only store a reference pointing to the data. 
   */
